00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:18.233
The first tip that I realized was
extremely necessary when testing my

00:04:18.233 --> 00:04:23.493
own server on the cloud
is to make sure you test

00:04:23.493 --> 00:04:26.367
every case possibly with cases that fail.

00:04:26.587 --> 00:04:31.487
So send it on purpose on every run
because you're currently changing

00:04:31.487 --> 00:04:36.827
your own workflows for example via
vibe coding like you're chatting with

00:04:36.827 --> 00:04:40.507
basically how does this look in practice
you're chatting with Claude and you say

00:04:40.507 --> 00:04:44.367
okay this i want the workflow i want to
add this feature to the workflow i want to

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:49.133
add whatever and Claude will change your
workflows and as a measure of safety,

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:55.300
for every run you have this gate
check where it purposefully sends a

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:58.380
row that must fail and one
that must pass the gate.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:04.080
If there was any alteration to the gate
that makes it either block both rows or

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:09.060
pass both rows, it will block the system
and email you so that you know something is

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:14.133
wrong. So you need to set up these
gates in order to ensure your whole

00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:19.573
system won't harm you in any way or
do something that's not up to your

00:05:19.573 --> 00:05:24.800
own expectations. I have a few quick
examples that I'll just skip through quite

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.920
quickly. You can jump onto
