00:08:26.067 --> 00:08:30.527
So tip number two, your credentials
are part of the deploy and when you're

00:08:30.527 --> 00:08:35.847
hosting your own n8n server,
a lot of issues come about and

00:08:35.847 --> 00:08:40.447
I'm just giving you quick tips on which
issues these are and how I solve them.

00:08:40.647 --> 00:08:45.507
So when you re-import anything into your
n8n, your credentials are lost so they're

00:08:45.507 --> 00:08:47.873
not encrypted into the cloud itself.

00:08:47.873 --> 00:08:52.813
So a quick solution is to
simply put your credentials

00:08:52.813 --> 00:08:57.159
in your own file. Your OAuth

00:08:58.293 --> 00:09:01.833
app in Google only works when
it's sent to production.

00:09:02.273 --> 00:09:07.373
If you're not doing that, it just
happened to me and I realized that by

00:09:07.373 --> 00:09:11.633
working with this model after one
week, it simply stops working.

00:09:11.853 --> 00:09:14.213
So you start getting failed records.

00:09:14.213 --> 00:09:17.973
So make sure you put your
consent screen to production.

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:22.247
Then again on the second version of
n8n, it is very specific, you have to

00:09:22.247 --> 00:09:26.087
actually save. Like saving is not enough,
you have to publish your changes or

00:09:26.087 --> 00:09:28.907
otherwise it can really
induce you to errors.

00:09:29.127 --> 00:09:32.200
So you assume you're already
actually working and lost.

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:35.653
You assume the latest version is already
running when it's actually the oldest

00:09:35.653 --> 00:09:38.893
version running. So always
publish your changes.

00:09:38.893 --> 00:09:44.220
And last tip, make sure
AI is judging but your

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:49.640
model is strong enough to handle
most of the dirty work so I'm giving

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:54.927
you a few examples of how I set this
up. The way, or these specific workflows

00:09:55.067 --> 00:09:59.933
where this tip was most applicable was
specifically for fetching different

00:09:59.933 --> 00:10:05.233
emails and categorizing them,
and then sorting those emails and

00:10:05.233 --> 00:10:08.573
applying the sheet in which I
tracked all of my applications.

00:10:08.773 --> 00:10:14.173
And so this is a small trick that
I had to do in order to make

00:10:14.173 --> 00:10:19.200
the system work well. So for example, once
I was fetching 200 emails, I was getting

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:24.567
an error due to exceeding the
maximum amount of emails fetchable

00:10:24.567 --> 00:10:28.307
from Google, so I had
to cap it down to 60.

00:10:28.527 --> 00:10:33.427
I also noticed that some IDs were being
swapped, so emails were being confused,

00:10:33.707 --> 00:10:39.127
specific emails that had very long
IDs, so I basically added an echo for

00:10:39.127 --> 00:10:41.367
each subject and also numbered them.

00:10:41.647 --> 00:10:45.660
This is very specific, so this is my own
experience and probably something that

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:51.150
could happen with you if you happen to
also integrate a model that analyzes

00:10:51.433 --> 00:10:53.813
emails and try to make it
as efficient as possible.

00:10:53.967 --> 00:10:59.433
Again, as I mentioned earlier, I set
up the system to avoid running AI

00:10:59.433 --> 00:11:04.913
on emails that have already been sent
before and basically what it does is

00:11:04.913 --> 00:11:10.313
I have a memory that stores all the emails
that have already been analyzed by the

00:11:10.313 --> 00:11:15.787
AI, and that ensures that only new
emails that don't match the emails

00:11:15.787 --> 00:11:16.827
in the memory are analyzed.

00:11:17.087 --> 00:11:21.307
So this is a simple step that I'm pretty
sure most of you are already applying to

00:11:21.307 --> 00:11:26.227
your own processes. And in the end,
this is basically the structure

00:11:26.227 --> 00:11:28.667
for when the AI actually acts.

00:11:28.667 --> 00:11:33.973
So there's the gates, these things
are contained in the sheets

00:11:33.973 --> 00:11:38.627
where there's columns, I could show them
to you later if you want, come to me and

00:11:38.627 --> 00:11:41.007
I'll show you these sheets
with which I interact.

00:11:41.055 --> 00:11:43.953
They have OK columns, final email, etc.

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:49.407
The AI workflow that I
set up checks whether

00:11:49.407 --> 00:11:53.527
each box, each row has an
OK or does not have an OK.

00:11:53.787 --> 00:11:59.107
If it does have an OK, it sends them
to the AI guard to basically make sure

00:11:59.107 --> 00:12:01.647
that everything is actually into place.

00:12:01.647 --> 00:12:07.147
If there's anything I think with a specific
range within a specific interval of

00:12:07.147 --> 00:12:12.220
confidence that is off, in very simple terms,
it blocks the sending of the email, or

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:17.240
otherwise, if there's a confidence
interval above 70%, it sends the email.

